# Configuration lives in .env

Application configuration is read from the environment. Do not hardcode it in source.

This includes names and labels, RPC URLs, chain ids, private keys, contract addresses the app chooses, durations, payment-token choice, and feature flags.

```typescript
// Bad
const LABEL = "horrortube";
const RPC = "https://ethereum-sepolia-rpc.publicnode.com";

// Good
const label = process.env.ENS_LABEL;
if (label === undefined || label.trim() === "") {
  throw new Error("ENS_LABEL is required. Set it in .env. See .env.example.");
}
```

- Add every variable to `.env.example` with an empty value. Do not put secrets or real defaults there.
- If a variable is missing or blank, fail with an error that names the variable.
- `.env` stays gitignored. Never commit it.

Checked-in contract artifacts are not environment config. ABIs and the pinned deployment address table under `scripts/pin/` and `scripts/abis/` stay in the repo. The app still chooses which deployment, label, key, and RPC to use through `.env`.
